//! lotus/reverb_place.rye -- ALES214: REVERB PLACE -- reverberate the master WET, then SEAT the reverberated result in the field: how WIDE it sits
//! and WHERE, in one call. ALES212 answered how wide (reverb_width); ALES213 answered where (reverb_pan). A keeper mixing a stereo master reaches
//! for both at once -- seat the reverb return narrow and slightly left, or wide and centred -- yet each rung asked the other's work be run separately.
//! This rung is the capstone the width and pan sub-series earned: reverberate, then set the wet image's width AND position with a single seating.
//!
//! The shape, composing three proven stages with NO new audio arithmetic:
//!   1. validate the WIDTH fraction (BadWidth) AND the PAN fraction (BadPan) BEFORE any write -- a bad fraction leaves the master fully dry
//!   2. reverberate the master WET in place -- ALES191's `stereo_reverb_preset`, unchanged (it prechecks its own faults before any write)
//!   3. set how wide the wet sits -- ALES211's `stereo_width`, the width already proven legal so this stage cannot fault
//!   4. place the wet image -- read ALES11's equal-power split `[w_left, w_right]` for the position and scale each wet channel by its weight over the
//!      field denominator through ALES2's proven `timeline.gain`, the pan already proven legal so neither gain can fault
//! Both fractions are checked first so a bad seating never lets the reverb write: the op refuses whole up front (the master left dry) or completes
//! whole. One reverb, then two proven placements -- every sample it writes is written by a stage already proven byte for byte.
//!
//! The edges name the two knobs. `width == 0` (MONO COLLAPSE) folds the wet to its shared mid on both channels; `pan == den/2` (equal-power CENTRE)
//! then attenuates both equally, so a mono wash is held centred below the pure wet. `width == den` (UNITY) is the mid/side round-trip over the
//! reverberated master (ALES210's honest ≤ 1 LSB per sample); `pan == 0` (HARD LEFT) then routes the whole wash left (left scaled by den/den, right
//! silenced). Each pan weight is a non-negative amplitude no greater than den (ALES11's isqrt monotone, den² its cap), so the placement attenuates,
//! never amplifies.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES214.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-125233_fill-ales214-lotus-reverb-place.md
//! Stands on: lotus/stereo_reverb_preset.rye (ALES191) -- the named-room stereo wet, run over the master unchanged, its StereoReverbPresetError
//! forwarded whole; lotus/stereo_width.rye (ALES211) -- the width control (encode/scale-the-side/decode), run over the wet, its BadWidth reused; lotus/
//! power.rye (ALES11) -- the equal-power split `split(pos, den)` = [isqrt(den-(den−pos)), isqrt(den-pos)], reused verbatim; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2)
//! -- the Clip and its proven `gain`; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip, its balance invariant, and the fold bound max_pan_den; lotus/clock.rye
//! (ALES5) -- the validated sample clock the preset speaks its span against.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. One bounded in-process i16 StereoClip, a validated clock, ALES191's fixed banks, siloed to lotus/.
//! Reverb place is a pure composition of three proven stages over existing samples; it fabricates none, changes no length, and reads no byte past
//! either channel; it inherits ALES210's honest ≤ 1 LSB round-trip cost at unity width. The width and pan laws are routing weights, not psychoacoustic
//! or electrical claims. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/reverb_place.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const clock = @import("clock.rye");
const stereo_reverb_preset = @import("stereo_reverb_preset.rye");
const stereo_width = @import("stereo_width.rye");
const power = @import("power.rye");

pub const reverb_place_version = "20260815.125233";

/// The named rooms are ALES190's own, reached through ALES191 -- reused whole, so reverb place and the presets name the same tunings and cannot drift
/// apart.
pub const Room = stereo_reverb_preset.Room;

/// The width bounds are ALES211's own, reused whole so reverb place and the plain width control name the same span. A width is `num/den`: 0 is mono
/// (the wet's side zeroed), `den` is unity, past `den` widens.
pub const max_width_den: u32 = stereo_width.max_width_den;
pub const max_width_num: u32 = stereo_width.max_width_num;

/// The pan bound is ALES10's own fold bound, reached through ALES11 -- reused whole so reverb place and the plain pan name the same field. A pan is
/// `pos/den`: 0 is hard left, `den` is hard right, `den/2` is the equal-power centre.
pub const max_pan_den: u32 = power.max_pan_den;

/// Reverb place's faults are ALES191's `StereoReverbPresetError` (the clock's DurationTooLong and the reverb's own faults, forwarded whole) beside
/// two own faults -- `BadWidth` (a width with a zero or over-bound denominator or an over-bound numerator) and `BadPan` (a pan with a zero or
/// over-bound denominator or a position past the field) -- each refused by name before any write.
pub const ReverbPlaceError = stereo_reverb_preset.StereoReverbPresetError || error{ BadWidth, BadPan };

/// Reverberate `count_ms` of a stereo master `sc` from `start_ms` with a NAMED ROOM, then SEAT the reverberated result: scale the wet's SIDE by
/// `width_num/width_den` (how wide) and place the result at `pan_pos/pan_den` in the field (where) by scaling each wet channel by its equal-power
/// weight. `width_num == 0` collapses the wash to mono; `width_den == width_num` is unity (the mid/side round-trip, ≤ 1 LSB per sample). `pan_pos ==
/// 0` routes the whole wash LEFT (right silenced); `pan_pos == pan_den` routes it RIGHT; `pan_pos == pan_den/2` is the equal-power centre (both
/// channels ≈ 0.707-wet). Refuses BadWidth or BadPan on a bad fraction BEFORE the reverb writes, then forwards ALES191's StereoReverbPresetError
/// (DurationTooLong, BadRange) before any write; on refusal the master is left dry, untouched, and balanced.
pub fn reverb_place(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    clk: clock.Clock,
    room: Room,
    start_ms: u32,
    count_ms: u32,
    width_num: u32,
    width_den: u32,
    pan_pos: u32,
    pan_den: u32,
) ReverbPlaceError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant the shared preset, width, and gains preserve.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    // invariant: the clock is valid -- the span is spoken against it, and stereo_reverb_preset asserts the same.
    assert(clk.rate >= 1 and clk.rate <= clock.max_rate);
    // invariant: the width is a real, bounded fraction -- checked BEFORE the reverb writes so a bad width never leaves a wet, un-seated master. The
    // same bounds ALES211 checks, so the width stage below cannot fault.
    if (width_den == 0 or width_den > max_width_den or width_num > max_width_num) return error.BadWidth;
    // invariant: the pan is a real, bounded fraction -- checked BEFORE the reverb writes too. A zero denominator is no fraction, a position past the
    // field no place in it; the denominator is bounded to ALES11's fold bound so the equal-power split stays within its proven range.
    if (pan_den == 0 or pan_den > max_pan_den or pan_pos > pan_den) return error.BadPan;

    const n: u32 = sc.left.len;

    // 1. Reverberate the master WET in place -- ALES191's stereo_reverb_preset, unchanged. It prechecks DurationTooLong / BadRange / its own reverb
    //    faults before any write, so a reverb fault here leaves the master dry and balanced (and both fractions already proven legal above).
    try stereo_reverb_preset.stereo_reverb_preset(sc, clk, room, start_ms, count_ms);

    // 2. Set how wide the reverberated wet sits -- ALES211's stereo_width, the width proven legal above so it cannot raise BadWidth here; the catch
    //    re-raises as our own fault, yet the invariant is that it never fires.
    stereo_width.stereo_width(sc, width_num, width_den) catch {
        // invariant: unreachable -- the width is prechecked legal with ALES211's own bounds, so stereo_width cannot fault here.
        return error.BadWidth;
    };

    // 3. Read ALES11's equal-power split for the proven-legal position -- [w_left, w_right], each a non-negative amplitude ≤ pan_den (isqrt monotone,
    //    pan_den² its cap). No pan arithmetic is re-derived here; the split is spent verbatim.
    const w = power.split(pan_pos, pan_den);
    // invariant: each weight is a legal attenuation numerator -- no greater than the field denominator, so each channel gain is a fraction of the wet
    //   and the gain's saturate is a proven no-op (the placement attenuates, never amplifies).
    assert(w[0] <= pan_den and w[1] <= pan_den);

    // 4. Scale each wet channel by its weight over the field denominator -- ALES2's proven gain, saturating once. The span is the full valid clip and
    //    the denominator is proven non-zero above, so neither gain can raise BadGain or BadRange; the catch re-raises as our own fault, never fires.
    timeline.gain(&sc.left, 0, n, @intCast(w[0]), pan_den) catch {
        // invariant: unreachable -- the pan is prechecked legal and the span is the whole clip, so gain cannot fault here.
        return error.BadPan;
    };
    timeline.gain(&sc.right, 0, n, @intCast(w[1]), pan_den) catch {
        // invariant: unreachable -- as above, for the right channel.
        return error.BadPan;
    };

    // invariant: reverb place writes values only -- both channels keep their length, so the master leaves balanced.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len and sc.left.len == n);
}

// --- selftest: THE WIDTH-AT-CENTRE LAW (width == 0, pan centre) -- both channels equal (mono), each an attenuation of the reverberated shared mid;
//     THE PAN-AT-UNITY LAW (width == unity, pan == 0) -- the right silenced, the left the unity-width wet placed left; THE COMPOSITION LAW -- a general
//     width and off-centre pan equals stereo_reverb_preset then stereo_width then the two equal-power gains byte for byte, each channel an attenuation
//     of the widened wet; THE CENTRED-INVARIANT LAW -- a centred master seated at centre keeps left == right, each attenuated below the pure wet; THE
//     FAULT LAW -- a bad width BadWidth, a bad pan BadPan, a span past the clip DurationTooLong, an out-of-range span BadRange, each leaving the master
//     dry and untouched --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn abs16(x: i16) i32 {
    const v: i32 = x;
    return if (v < 0) -v else v;
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const c = try clock.make(1000); // 1 sample per ms — each ms delay is one sample, the bench legible
    // Two distinct impulses so the wet channels genuinely differ (no crossing can hide), each long enough for the hall's strongest comb to decay.
    const left = blk: {
        var b: [1024]i16 = [_]i16{0} ** 1024;
        b[0] = 12000;
        break :blk b;
    };
    const right = blk: {
        var b: [1024]i16 = [_]i16{0} ** 1024;
        b[3] = 9000; // a different impulse at a different position — the right channel is not the left
        break :blk b;
    };

    // The pure stereo reverb -- the wet every edge reshapes. Computed once so each edge reads against the same wash.
    var wet = pan.StereoClip{};
    try load_stereo(&wet, &left, &right);
    try stereo_reverb_preset.stereo_reverb_preset(&wet, c, .hall, 0, 1024);

    // 1. THE WIDTH-AT-CENTRE LAW -- width == 0 collapses the wet to its shared mid on both channels; pan == den/2 (centre) then attenuates both
    //    equally, so the result is MONO (left == right) and each channel an attenuation of the collapsed mid.
    {
        // the reference: the pure reverb, collapsed to mono (width 0), then the two equal-power centre gains.
        var ref = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&ref, &left, &right);
        try stereo_reverb_preset.stereo_reverb_preset(&ref, c, .hall, 0, 1024);
        try stereo_width.stereo_width(&ref, 0, 4096);
        const mid = ref.left.samples(); // after collapse both channels are the shared mid; read it before the pan attenuates

        var clip = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&clip, &left, &right);
        try reverb_place(&clip, c, .hall, 0, 1024, 0, 4096, 2048, 4096); // width 0 (mono), pan centre
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), clip.right.samples())) return error.WidthCentreNotMono;
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < mid.len) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(clip.left.samples()[i]) > abs16(mid[i])) return error.WidthCentreNotAttenuated;
        }
    }

    // 2. THE PAN-AT-UNITY LAW -- width == den (unity) is the mid/side round-trip over the reverberated master, so the wet keeps essentially its stereo
    //    image (≤ 1 LSB per sample); pan == 0 then routes the whole wash left -- the left is the unity-width wet placed left (identity gain), the right
    //    silenced.
    {
        var ref = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&ref, &left, &right);
        try stereo_reverb_preset.stereo_reverb_preset(&ref, c, .hall, 0, 1024);
        try stereo_width.stereo_width(&ref, 4096, 4096); // unity: the mid/side round-trip
        const unity_left = ref.left.samples();

        var clip = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&clip, &left, &right);
        try reverb_place(&clip, c, .hall, 0, 1024, 4096, 4096, 0, 2); // width unity, pan hard left
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), unity_left)) return error.PanUnityLeftNotWet;
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < clip.right.samples().len) : (i += 1) if (clip.right.samples()[i] != 0) return error.PanUnityRightNotSilent;
    }

    // 3. THE COMPOSITION LAW -- a general width and off-centre pan: reverb_place IS reverberate-then-widen-then-place, so its result equals ALES191's
    //    reverb over a fresh copy, then ALES211's width, then ALES11's equal-power gains, BYTE FOR BYTE on both channels; and each channel is an
    //    attenuation of the WIDENED wet (each pan weight ≤ den, so the gain never amplifies).
    {
        var clip = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&clip, &left, &right);
        try reverb_place(&clip, c, .hall, 0, 1024, 6000, 4096, 1000, 4096); // wider than unity, off-centre pan

        // the reference: the pure reverb, then the width, then the two equal-power gains -- exactly the stages reverb_place composes.
        var ref = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&ref, &left, &right);
        try stereo_reverb_preset.stereo_reverb_preset(&ref, c, .hall, 0, 1024);
        try stereo_width.stereo_width(&ref, 6000, 4096);
        const widened_l = ref.left.samples();
        const widened_r = ref.right.samples();
        // snapshot the widened wet before the gains attenuate it, to check the placement never amplifies.
        var wl_copy: [1024]i16 = undefined;
        var wr_copy: [1024]i16 = undefined;
        var k: u32 = 0;
        while (k < widened_l.len) : (k += 1) {
            wl_copy[k] = widened_l[k];
            wr_copy[k] = widened_r[k];
        }
        const w = power.split(1000, 4096);
        try timeline.gain(&ref.left, 0, ref.left.len, @intCast(w[0]), 4096);
        try timeline.gain(&ref.right, 0, ref.right.len, @intCast(w[1]), 4096);

        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), ref.left.samples())) return error.CompositionLeftBroken;
        if (!eq(clip.right.samples(), ref.right.samples())) return error.CompositionRightBroken;

        // each channel is an attenuation of the widened wet -- the placement seats, it does not amplify.
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < clip.left.samples().len) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(clip.left.samples()[i]) > abs16(wl_copy[i])) return error.LeftAmplified;
            if (abs16(clip.right.samples()[i]) > abs16(wr_copy[i])) return error.RightAmplified;
        }
    }

    // 4. THE CENTRED-INVARIANT LAW -- a centred master (left == right) reverberates through one shared bank to a centred wet whose side is zero, so
    //    width leaves it untouched; a centre pan then keeps the two channels identical, each attenuated below the pure wet.
    {
        var centred_wet = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&centred_wet, &left, &left);
        try stereo_reverb_preset.stereo_reverb_preset(&centred_wet, c, .hall, 0, 1024);
        const cwl = centred_wet.left.samples();

        var clip = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&clip, &left, &left);
        try reverb_place(&clip, c, .hall, 0, 1024, 8192, 4096, 2048, 4096); // widen (no-op on a centred wet), pan centre
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), clip.right.samples())) return error.CentreNotSymmetric;
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < cwl.len) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(clip.left.samples()[i]) > abs16(cwl[i])) return error.CentreNotAttenuated;
        }
    }

    // 5. THE FAULT LAW -- a bad width, a bad pan, a span past the clip, and an out-of-range span each refuse by name BEFORE the reverb writes, so the
    //    master is left DRY, untouched, and balanced.
    {
        var clip = pan.StereoClip{};
        const threel = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        const threer = [_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 };
        try load_stereo(&clip, &threel, &threer);
        // a zero width denominator is no width -- BadWidth, before the reverb writes, the master left dry.
        if (reverb_place(&clip, c, .room, 0, 3, 1, 0, 1, 2)) |_| return error.ZeroWidthSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadWidth) return error.WrongZeroWidth;
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), &threel) or !eq(clip.right.samples(), &threer)) return error.ZeroWidthMutated;
        // an over-bound width numerator -- BadWidth, master dry.
        if (reverb_place(&clip, c, .room, 0, 3, max_width_num + 1, 4096, 1, 2)) |_| return error.OverWidthSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadWidth) return error.WrongOverWidth;
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), &threel) or !eq(clip.right.samples(), &threer)) return error.OverWidthMutated;
        // a zero pan denominator is no pan -- BadPan, master dry (the width is legal, so the pan check is what refuses).
        if (reverb_place(&clip, c, .room, 0, 3, 4096, 4096, 1, 0)) |_| return error.ZeroPanSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPan) return error.WrongZeroPan;
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), &threel) or !eq(clip.right.samples(), &threer)) return error.ZeroPanMutated;
        // a pan position past the field -- BadPan, master dry.
        if (reverb_place(&clip, c, .room, 0, 3, 4096, 4096, 3, 2)) |_| return error.OverPanSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPan) return error.WrongOverPan;
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), &threel) or !eq(clip.right.samples(), &threer)) return error.OverPanMutated;
        // a count past max_clip converts past the clip bound -- DurationTooLong from the clock, before any write.
        if (reverb_place(&clip, c, .room, 0, timeline.max_clip + 1, 4096, 4096, 1, 2)) |_| return error.LongSpanSlipped else |e| if (e != error.DurationTooLong) return error.WrongLongSpan;
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), &threel) or !eq(clip.right.samples(), &threer)) return error.LongSpanMutated;
        // an out-of-range span (2 ms start, 5 ms count past a 3-sample clip) refuses BadRange before any write.
        if (reverb_place(&clip, c, .room, 2, 5, 4096, 4096, 1, 2)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), &threel) or !eq(clip.right.samples(), &threer)) return error.BadRangeMutated;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-reverb-place: REVERB PLACE — reverberate the master WET, then SEAT the reverberated result in the field: how WIDE it sits and " ++
        "WHERE, in one call, the capstone the ALES212 width and ALES213 pan sub-series earned. A keeper mixing a stereo master reaches for both at " ++
        "once — narrow and slightly left, or wide and centred — yet each rung asked the other's work be run separately. The shape composes three " ++
        "proven stages with NO new audio arithmetic: validate the width AND the pan first (a bad fraction refuses BadWidth or BadPan before the " ++
        "reverb writes), reverberate wet in place (ALES191's stereo_reverb_preset, unchanged), set how wide (ALES211's stereo_width, the width " ++
        "proven legal so it cannot fault), place the wet image (ALES11's equal-power split feeding ALES2's proven gain on each channel, the pan " ++
        "proven legal so neither gain can fault). THE WIDTH-AT-CENTRE LAW: width == 0 with a centre pan folds the wet to mono (left == right), each " ++
        "channel an attenuation of the reverberated shared mid. THE PAN-AT-UNITY LAW: unity width keeps the wet's stereo image (the mid/side " ++
        "round-trip, ≤ 1 LSB per sample), a hard-left pan then routes the whole wash left with the right silenced. THE COMPOSITION LAW: a general " ++
        "width and off-centre pan equals the reverb then the width then the two equal-power gains byte for byte, each channel an attenuation of the " ++
        "widened wet. THE CENTRED-INVARIANT LAW: a centred master reverberates to a centred wet whose side is zero (width untouched), and a centre " ++
        "pan keeps left == right, each attenuated below the wet. THE FAULT LAW: a bad width BadWidth, a bad pan BadPan, a span past the clip " ++
        "DurationTooLong, an out-of-range span BadRange, each leaving the master DRY and untouched. Each pan weight ≤ den, so the placement " ++
        "attenuates, never amplifies. Purely local: one StereoClip, a validated clock, ALES191's fixed banks; no network, no keys, no funds, no " ++
        "real device, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
